# Rank 1085 / CVE-2021-33564: Dragonfly's URL-driven job API let a request
# specify the :convert processor with a raw argument string, and that string
# reached ImageMagick's convert command shell-escaped per word but never
# constrained against a leading dash, so a value like "-write /var/www/shell.rb"
# is still one shell word and still an ImageMagick option (CWE-88, CWE-94). Every
# word is individually shell-escaped in lib/dragonfly/shell.rb:
#
#   def escape_args(args)
#     args.shellsplit.map{|arg| escape(arg) }.join(' ')
#   end
#
# which stops shell metacharacters, not ImageMagick's own flag parsing, and this
# only fires when the app disables verify_url so the job is not signature-checked.
# The fix 25399297bb removes the :convert generator and processor from the
# registered set entirely, and replaces the :rotate shortcut's raw string
# interpolation with ParamValidators.validate!(amount, &ParamValidators.is_number)
# before Commands.convert(content, "-rotate #{amount}"), so no URL-reachable
# processor accepts an unconstrained argument string any more.
#
# ATTENTION, and it separates into two findings, one from rank 1050's cause and
# one new.
#
#  - Ruby has no argument-injection vocabulary at all. code.CommandOptionInjectionSink
#    and code.CommandOptionInjection are each bound in Go, Python, PHP and
#    JavaScript and not in Ruby, and even where they are bound, rank 1050 already
#    recorded that no rule in vyql/packs consumes CommandOptionInjectionSink, so
#    argument injection cannot be reported by taint in any language. This rank
#    adds that Ruby is missing at the binding layer too, on top of that.
#  - core.ShellEscape does not survive the map-then-join idiom that per-word
#    escaping requires. Shellwords.escape(x) called directly is recognized and
#    suppresses VYQL-INJ-002; the same call inside
#    args.map{|a| Shellwords.escape(a)}.join(' '), which is the shape Dragonfly's
#    own escape_args uses and the standard way to escape an array of shell words
#    in Ruby, is not recognized and the same taint reports. The binding's covers
#    path is call-to-call, and the join reconstructs a new value the dominance
#    check does not follow back through. This is a precision cost on correct
#    code rather than a missed detection, so it is recorded but not swept here.
#
# Scanning the plugin, processor, content, shell and job files together reports 2
# findings at the vulnerable revision: VYQL-CRY-001 on a WeakHash flag unrelated
# to this CVE, and VYQL-INJ-002 on shell.rb's own run_command treated as a public
# library entry point, which is the library-profile heuristic rather than the
# real URL-to-convert flow. Neither is this CVE, and the real flow is not traced:
# Job.deserialize turns a URL-encoded job spec into dynamic method dispatch, so
# the processor name and its args never reach shell_update through a resolved
# call chain.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap

test "rank1085: a url-controlled argument string reaches convert unconstrained"
  lang ruby
  file lib/dragonfly/image_magick/processors/convert.rb
  reject VYQL-INJ-031
  code
  ```
  module Dragonfly
    module ImageMagick
      module Processors
        class Convert
          def call(content, args = "", opts = {})
            convert_command = content.env[:convert_command] || "convert"
            format = opts["format"]

            content.shell_update ext: format do |old_path, new_path|
              "#{convert_command} #{args} #{old_path} #{new_path}"
            end

            if format
              content.meta["format"] = format.to_s
              content.ext = format
            end
          end
        end
      end
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1085: the fix drops raw args and validates the one shortcut kept"
  lang ruby
  file lib/dragonfly/image_magick/plugin.rb
  reject VYQL-INJ-031
  code
  ```
  module Dragonfly
    module ImageMagick
      class Plugin
        def call(app, opts = {})
          app.env[:convert_command] = opts[:convert_command] || "convert"

          app.add_processor :encode, Processors::Encode.new
          app.add_processor :thumb, Processors::Thumb.new
          app.add_processor :rotate do |content, amount|
            ParamValidators.validate!(amount, &ParamValidators.is_number)
            Commands.convert(content, "-rotate #{amount}")
          end
        end
      end
    end
  end
  ```

# The dominance pair. Shellwords.escape is bound as core.ShellEscape and
# suppresses called directly. Wrapped in the map-then-join idiom Dragonfly's own
# escape_args uses, the same value still reaches the sink and still reports.

test "rank1085: a direct Shellwords.escape call suppresses the finding"
  lang ruby
  file direct.rb
  reject VYQL-INJ-002
  code
  ```
  require "shellwords"
  require "open3"

  class ImageController < ApplicationController
    def show
      args = params[:args]
      escaped = Shellwords.escape(args)
      command = "convert #{escaped} #{path} #{new_path}"
      Open3.capture3(command)
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1085: the same escape wrapped in map-then-join does not"
  lang ruby
  file mapped.rb
  expect VYQL-INJ-002
  code
  ```
  require "shellwords"
  require "open3"

  class ImageController < ApplicationController
    def show
      args = params[:args]
      escaped = args.shellsplit.map { |a| Shellwords.escape(a) }.join(" ")
      command = "convert #{escaped} #{path} #{new_path}"
      Open3.capture3(command)
    end
  end
  ```
